===Verb===
{{en-verb}} {{tlb|en|chiefly|American}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To think of highly, to respect highly; to show respect for; to recognise the importance or spiritual value of.
#: {{syn|en|esteem|laud|worthy|Thesaurus:glorify|Thesaurus:revere}}
#: {{ant|en|despise|condemn}}
#: {{ux|en|The freedom fighters will be forever remembered and '''honored''' by the people.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Aly Tamboura and Kevin D. Sawyer|title=Black Panthers' militancy now adopted by the radical right|journal=w:San Francisco Chronicle|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20210612174150/https://www.sfchronicle.com/opinion/article/Black-Panthers-militancy-now-adopted-by-the-6808762.php|date=5 February 2016
|passage=Black history narratives '''honor''' the likes of Harriet Tubman, who formed the Underground Railroad, Frederick Douglass, who fought to abolish slavery, and Martin Luther King Jr., icon of the civil rights movement.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[conform]] to, [[abide]] by, act in [[accordance]] with (an agreement, treaty, promise, request, or the like).
#: {{ux|en|I trusted you, but you have not '''honored''' your promise.}}
#: {{ux|en|Our wishes were '''honored''' by changing the date of the wedding.}}
#: {{ux|en|Some application software does not '''honor''' the theme colors chosen in the operating system settings.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To confer ([[bestow]]) an honour or privilege upon (someone).
#: {{syn|en|dignify|elevate|uplift|Thesaurus:aggrandize}}
#: {{ux|en|Ten members of the profession were '''honored''' at the ceremony.}}
#: {{ux|en|The prince '''honored''' me with an invitation to his birthday banquet.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make payment in respect of (a cheque, banker's draft, etc.).
#: {{ux|en|I'm sorry Sir, but the bank did not '''honour''' your cheque.}}

